id: k8s.cron-job
kind: kubernetes
title: CronJob
aliases: cronjob, scheduled job
tags: workloads, batch, schedule
questions:
  - how do you schedule a recurring task?
invariant: A CronJob creates a Job on a cron schedule, with an explicit concurrency and history policy.
answer_30s: CronJob is a scheduler over Job objects: a cron expression creates Jobs, and concurrencyPolicy decides what happens when the previous run is still going.
answer_2m: |
  The important knobs are the failure semantics rather than the schedule:
  concurrencyPolicy Allow/Forbid/Replace decides overlap, startingDeadlineSeconds
  decides whether a missed window is still launched, and the Job's own
  backoffLimit and activeDeadlineSeconds decide retries. A CronJob does not
  replay missed windows unless a deadline is configured, which is the usual
  follow-up question after an outage.
when_to_use:
  - periodic cleanup, report generation, cache warming
avoid_when:
  - work that must be exactly-once (cron is at-most-once per window; make the task idempotent)
commands:
  - kubectl get cronjobs -A => schedule, last schedule time, suspended state
  - kubectl get jobs --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp => which scheduled run produced which Job
failure_modes:
  - overlapping runs mutating the same data (the default concurrencyPolicy is Allow)
  - missed windows after control-plane downtime
related:
  - k8s.job
  - design.idempotency
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/cron-jobs/
